<h3>Step 1 — writing the intent sentence</h3>
<p>This is the most important habit. Write it not as <strong>"do this for me" (a command)</strong> but as <strong>"we would like to do this" (an intent)</strong>. Only then does the harness have room to design the team itself. After typing <code>/harness</code>, autocomplete with <span class="kbd">Tab</span> and then append your request.</p>
<pre><code>/harness In the ERP sales/order module, we would like to proceed with a client
customization that automatically puts orders exceeding the credit limit per the
account's credit grade on hold and sends a notification to the sales rep.
Please include standard source impact analysis and regression tests as well.</code></pre>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Tips for giving a good intent (essential for beginners)</span>
    If you pack ① the <strong>goal</strong> (what) · ② the <strong>target module</strong> (where) · ③ the <strong>constraints</strong> (minimize damage to the standard · upgrade compatibility) · ④ the <strong>deliverables you want</strong> (impact · tests) into a single sentence, the team composition comes out accurate. For detailed background see <a href="#!hnp-agents-skills">Agent · skill design</a>.
</div>

<h3>Steps 2–3 — domain analysis &amp; team proposal</h3>
<p>When you press Enter, the harness <strong>analyzes the domain</strong> and along the way asks you to choose between things like <strong>"build the full harness / build only the harness"</strong>. If you have a standard specification or requirements document to refer to, you can feed it in at this point (it handles even hundreds-of-pages PDFs). Right after that it proposes a <strong>draft agent team</strong>. For example:</p>
<div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Proposed agent</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Sales standard-source analyst</td><td>Grasps the credit and order flow and the scope of impact of the customization</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Customization designer</td><td>Designs an extension approach that does not modify the standard directly (e.g. an add-on)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Development agent</td><td>Implements the hold handling and notification logic</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Regression QA agent</td><td>Verifies that the standard order and credit features are not broken</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h3>Step 4 — review, then "go ahead"</h3>
<p>Here <strong>a person steps in</strong>. You look at whether the proposed team is appropriate, and if it falls short you ask for changes. If it is fine, you approve with one word.</p>
<pre><code>go ahead</code></pre>
<p>From this moment the work <strong>leaves human hands</strong> and proceeds in the order the orchestrator planned.</p>

<h3>Step 5 — parallel autonomous execution (observing)</h3>
<ul>
    <li>The agents work <strong>in parallel</strong>. With <span class="kbd">Shift + arrow keys</span> you can peek into what each agent is doing (an agent that is working is marked with color).</li>
    <li>If you want to run another task at the same time, open <strong>one more terminal window</strong> and run a separate harness.</li>
    <li>The <strong>loop</strong> of development → QA → (when a problem is found) rework turns automatically.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Step 6 — checking the deliverables</h3>
<p>When it finishes, a <code>_workspace</code> folder appears in the project and documents like the ones below pile up (the names are examples to aid understanding; in reality the harness names them as planned).</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Requirements write-up</strong> — organizes the CR into a developable specification</li>
    <li><strong>Standard source impact</strong> — which standard features and screens are affected</li>
    <li><strong>Customization design</strong> — an extension approach that does not modify the standard directly, with upgrade compatibility considered</li>
    <li><strong>List of implementation changes</strong> — what was changed and how</li>
    <li><strong>Regression test results</strong> — classified as critical / major / minor</li>
    <li><strong>Migration guide</strong> — the procedure and cautions for applying it to production</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Remember — it is a draft</span>
    A harness result is <strong>not a finished product but a well-prepared draft</strong>. For ERP customization in particular, <strong>a person must always review the standard impact and the regression results</strong> before applying it. Ways to make verification more solid are covered in <a href="#!hnp-agents-skills">Verification · quality</a>. For what happened inside, see how it works.
</div>

